Hidden Cobra has been weaponizing a FASTCash‑related malware family in a sustained campaign against the financial sector that dates back to at least late 2016 and continued through 2017 and 2018.  The group’s primary focus has been banks in Africa and Asia as well as retail payment systems, leveraging the trojan’s ability to hijack ATM networks and execute rapid, high‑volume cash‑withdrawal operations.  In 2017 the attackers orchestrated simultaneous withdrawals from ATMs in more than 30 countries, and a year later repeated the feat in 23 countries, stealing tens of millions of dollars in aggregate.  Although no specific CVEs have been publicly linked to the malicious code, the operational tradecraft points to a highly modular loader that injects banking‑trojan components, captures POS credentials, and uses hard‑coded command‑and‑control (C2) endpoints to coordinate the mass withdrawals.  The campaign’s success hinges on the rapid propagation of the payload across interconnected payment networks, allowing the adversary to exploit weak transaction monitoring and inadequate segmentation between front‑end ATM controllers and back‑office processing systems.  Defensive teams should treat any anomalous ATM activity—especially clusters of withdrawals that occur within a narrow time window across disparate geographies—as a high‑severity incident.  Immediate mitigations include flagging and reporting suspicious activity to the DHS National Cybersecurity and Communications Integration Center (NCCIC) or the FBI’s CyberWatch program, isolating affected terminals, and deploying enhanced network‑segmentation controls.  Continuous monitoring for known IOCs, strict credential hygiene for POS and ATM administration accounts, and regular threat‑intelligence updates are essential to blunt Hidden Cobra’s financially motivated operations and reduce the risk of further multi‑country cash‑out attacks.

