Draft:
On October 11 2017 a critical deadline was reached for organizations that rely on DNSSEC to protect their domain name infrastructure. The root zone’s Key Signing Key (KSK) was scheduled to be rolled over, and any DNS resolver, registrar or DNS operator that had not updated its trust anchor to the new KSK would suddenly begin to fail validation of DNSSEC‑signed responses. This could manifest as intermittent resolution failures, inaccessible web services, or broken email delivery for affected domains, effectively disrupting normal business operations. The impact is not limited to any particular sector; any entity that publishes or consumes DNSSEC‑signed zones—ranging from financial institutions to government agencies and small‑to‑medium businesses—could experience service degradation. The mitigation is straightforward: ensure that all DNS infrastructure components are configured to trust the new KSK before the October 11 deadline. Administrators should verify that their recursive resolvers, authoritative servers, and any third‑party DNS services have applied the updated trust anchor, and perform post‑rollover testing to confirm continued resolution integrity.

